<TopicPage slug="subjects/history/intermediate/the-league-of-nations">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Option B’s second key question — <strong>to what extent was the League of Nations a
    success?</strong> — is deliberately a two-sided one, and the focus points show you how to
    argue it: judge the League’s organisation and membership, its 1920s peacekeeping record,
    its humanitarian work, and finally what the Depression did to it in the 1930s. The honest
    answer is that <strong>the League was a working machine that the great powers stopped
    operating</strong> — and your essay grade depends on saying precisely where each half of
    that sentence is true.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="how the League was built — and where it was weak" widget="equationStep" config={machineCfg} />

  <h2>The machine and its missing parts</h2>
  <p>
    Read the structure as a set of promises, then check who could break them. The
    <strong>Council</strong> met several times a year and could call sanctions; the
    <strong>Assembly</strong> — every member one vote, meeting each September — was a world
    parliament in miniature; the <strong>Secretariat</strong> did the clerical work of a
    foreign ministry for the world; the <strong>Court</strong> at The Hague settled legal
    disputes. Article 16 of the Covenant promised collective security: if a member went to
    war in breach of the rules, every other member would cut its trade and, if needed,
    contribute troops. Now the absences. The United States, whose president had invented the
    thing, never joined after the Senate rejected the treaty in March 1920. Soviet Russia,
    treated as a pariah, stayed out until 1934. Germany was kept out until 1926. Japan and
    Italy sat as permanent Council members while plotting the very aggressions the League
    existed to stop — and the Depression then gave all of them a reason to look inward at
    the exact moment the rules required looking outward. The machine was built; the crew
    walked away from the controls.
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="peacekeeping and its collapse, 1920–46 — chips separate the decades" widget="timeline" config={timelineCfg} />

  <h2>The 1920s: small states, real successes</h2>
  <p>
    In the decade the League is mocked for, compare its failures and successes carefully.
    <strong>Vilna (1920)</strong> failed because Poland was willing to defy the League and the
    great powers were not willing to force it. But the <strong>Aaland Islands (1921)</strong>,
    <strong>Upper Silesia (1921)</strong> and the <strong>Greece–Bulgaria dispute (1925)</strong>
    were settled by arbitration, partition and ordered withdrawal — with compliance. The
    pattern is stark: the League could handle any dispute <em>between states that wanted
    resolving</em> and could not handle one involving a great power with troops — as Corfu
    (1923), resolved around the League by the Conference of Ambassadors, demonstrated. Its
    humanitarian record is easier to defend still: hundreds of thousands of prisoners of war
    repatriated after 1918, the Nansen passport for stateless refugees, the Health
    Organisation’s campaigns against typhus and malaria, the ILO’s drive for a 48-hour week,
    and the 1926 slavery convention — work that saved lives even in the years the
    peacekeeping made headlines for failing.
  </p>

  <h2>The 1930s: the Depression breaks it</h2>
  <p>
    The focus point about the Depression is the key to the failures. After 1929, governments
    everywhere raised tariffs and grabbed what they could; cooperation became expensive just
    when it was most needed, and the two crises of the decade hit a League whose strongest
    members were bankrupt in everything but arms. In <strong>Manchuria (1931–33)</strong>,
    Japan — a permanent Council member — conquered a province larger than France and Germany combined; the Lytton
    Commission took over a year to report, the Assembly condemned, and Japan simply resigned.
    Nobody proposed sanctions: Britain’s navy was far away and the Depression made trade war
    unthinkable. In <strong>Abyssinia (1935–36)</strong>, Britain and France half-heartedly
    sanctioned Italy while keeping the Suez Canal open to its supply ships and scheming
    (Hoare-Laval) to give Mussolini most of what he wanted; when Addis Ababa fell, sanctions
    were dropped. Every dictator learned the same lesson — and Hitler tested it by
    remilitarising the Rhineland in March 1936, in the middle of the crisis. By the late
    1930s the League was a bureaucracy without a function. The verdict your exam wants:
    weaknesses of organisation and membership made failure <em>likely</em>, not inevitable —
    the League succeeded exactly as far as its strongest members believed in it, and no
    further.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Cambridge IGCSE History 0470 syllabus (2027–2028), Core Option B key question 2; Northedge, The League of Nations: Its Life and Times; Henig, The League of Nations.</p>
